<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>AgentMemory.LongMemEval</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    AgentEval is consumed as a PACKAGE, not as a source ProjectReference.

    The reference used to be `$(AgentEvalSourceRoot)\src\AgentEval.Memory\...csproj` with
    AgentEvalSourceRoot defaulting to a hardcoded absolute path. Both this project and its test
    project are listed in AgentMemory.slnx, and CI runs `dotnet build AgentMemory.slnx`, so that
    default broke the build on any machine without that exact folder — including every CI runner.

    A package also makes a benchmark run reproducible: the evaluator is version-pinned rather than
    being whatever happens to sit in a sibling working tree. The LongMemEval types this harness uses
    (IEvaluableAgent, IHistoryInjectableAgent, ISessionResettableAgent, ExternalBenchmarkResult,
    QuestionEvidenceEnvelope) all ship inside the AgentEval umbrella package: AgentEval.Memory is
    IsPackable=false and is bundled via ProjectReference PrivateAssets="all" in AgentEval.csproj.
    This harness uses only AgentEval's public API — no InternalsVisibleTo grant exists — so nothing
    here depends on source access.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- 29.1. Transitive, and vulnerable at the version net10.0 resolves (9.0.4, GHSA-73j8-2gch-69rq).
         Pinned explicitly rather than suppressed: NU1903 is a real advisory, and NoWarn would
         hide the next one too. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Bcl.Memory" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      0.23.0-beta is the v3-corpora TypedMemEval release (ADR-026 family; v1's corpora had
      gold-separability tells — V7 probe — and must not be cited). Nothing in this harness
      hard-codes a corpus SHA-256 or question count — counts and identifiers are read from
      TypedMemEvalCorpus/TypedMemEvalVerticals at run time, which is what made this a one-line bump.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AgentEval" Version="0.29.0-beta" />
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.AI.OpenAI" Version="2.8.0-beta.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.AI.OpenAI" Version="10.6.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting" Version="10.0.10" />
    <!--
      A real tokenizer, for the publication path only. The chars/4 heuristic stays for RATIOS between
      arms, where a consistent approximation is sufficient and cheap. It is not sufficient for a
      number we publish: a reader will compare a published token count against provider billing.
      This is a tool, never packaged, so the dependency costs consumers nothing.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.Tokenizers" Version="2.0.0" />
    <!-- Embedded vocabularies, so counting never needs a network fetch at run time. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.Tokenizers.Data.O200kBase" Version="2.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.Tokenizers.Data.Cl100kBase" Version="2.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.Neo4j" Version="4.14.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\AgentMemory\AgentMemory.csproj" />
    <!--
      The MAF adapter is NOT part of the AgentMemory meta-package, so it has to be referenced
      explicitly. 7.6's procedural arm is a real agent reading real recalled memory, which means the
      shipped Neo4jMemoryContextProvider rather than a stand-in: a benchmark wired to a substitute
      would measure the substitute, and the gap this reference closed (no provider on either arm)
      voided five runs before it was noticed.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\AgentMemory.AgentFramework\AgentMemory.AgentFramework.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="AgentMemory.Tests.Unit.LongMemEval" />
  </ItemGroup>


  <!-- The memory-type mapping is embedded so it versions with the code that scores against it. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Taxonomy\memory-type-map.json" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
